  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: An elongated 500mb ridging pattern remains \npositioned over the western two-thirds of the country, with \ndownstream longwave troughing centered over Northern Quebec. An \nembedded shortwave trough currently over the Manitoba/Ontario border \nwith attached surface low pressure will dig across the Great Lakes \nRegion this afternoon through evening, producing widespread showers \nand thunderstorms tonight with enough moisture and instability. \nLatest guidance continues to support enough energy for severe storm \npotential followed by a frontal passage overnight that will draw a \ncool northerly air mass into the area.\n\n\nAforementioned longwave troughing will extend south of Michigan this \nTuesday, returning a cooler Canadian Air mass influence. \nTemperatures Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday are expected to remain \nbelow normal for late July with highs in the upper 60s to low 70s \nand breezy northwest flow. Chances of precipitation remain rather \nlimited beyond the midweek as Upstream midlevel subsidence supports \nsurface high pressure. A few embedded weak waves will likely support \nlight periods of scattered showers through the end of the week, but \nlittle to no QPF is expected at this time.  \n\n\nForecast Details:\n\nToday and tonight: The primary concern for the forecast rests on the \napproaching shortwave moving across Ontario today. Moisture \nadvection ahead of the front will drive dew points into the mid 60s \nalong with moderate instability produced via diurnal heating. \nShowers and storms will become more widespread tonight. Latest model \nsoundings depict a quasi \u201cinverted-V\u201d profile with adequate mid-\nlevel lapse rates and a strong low-level jet that favors any severe \nstorms producing damaging wind gusts. Severe potential aside, PWAT \nnear climatological maxima and skinny CAPE profiles could create \nlocalized heavy rainfall well over an inch for some parts of \nNorthern Lower this evening through the overnight hours.  \n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Cold air advection behind the shortwave will \nmove into the Great Lakes Region as the aforementioned deep \nmid/upper level trough dips its axis south of Michigan. Boundary \nlayer flow will keep conditions breezy this Tuesday and Wednesday \nwith gusts building around 30 knots. Temperatures will be noticeably \ncool as highs are only expected to climb into the upper 60s to low \n70s. While synoptic moisture will be rather limited behind Monday \nnight's frontal passage, cold 850mb temperatures will interact with \nthe warm lakes to keep low-level cloud cover across the CWA with \neven one or two locations typically affected by northwest flow \nobserving light showers/drizzle.\n \nThursday through Sunday Outlook:Surface high pressure builds firmly \nover the Great Lakes region by Thursday. Subsidence aloft will \ncreate light winds and temperatures slowly warming through the \nremainder of the forecast period as the core of the Canadian air \nmass shifts eastward. High temperatures will return toward the mid-\nto-upper 70s by Friday and approach climatological normal \ntemperatures around 80 degrees over the weekend. Overall quiet and \ndry weather will dominate the remainder of the forecast period. \nHowever, a few weak, moisture-starved waves pivoting around the \nnortheastern midlevel trough could bring brief periods of scattered \nshowers, with little to no QPF expected.",
